CONVERT-DOM
                     *****  Convert-Domain Program  *****

+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
! To get a list of all available commands, press SHIFT+HELP.           !
! To get help on one or more commands/topics, type help or press HELP. !
! To get help on one command, type the command name and press HELP.    !
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Convert-Domain is a program for converting domains in the old domain
format to new domains in the new domain format.

The main difference between the formats is that the old domain format
has a description file, while the new format does not.

You may also use the HELP command, which accepts advanced pattern
matching when searching for topics.

To get help on any command, write the name of the command on the
command line and then press HELP. This function
accepts ordinary SINTRAN-III matching rules.

Use SHIFT+HELP to get a list of all commands matching a given string.

Currently, the following topics are defined:

   COMMENT (topic)                        NEW-DOMAIN-FORMAT (topic)
   CONVERT-DOMAIN (command)               OLD-DOMAIN-FORMAT (topic)
   EXIT (command)                         SHELL (topic)
   HELP (command and default topic)       SIBAS (topic)
   LIMITATIONS (topic)

In addition, the following shell facilities are described:

   %        Shell comment  (NB: Use COMMENT as topic)
   @        Shell SINTRAN III command invocation

HELP
                              *****  Help  *****

Convert-Domain is a program for converting domains in the old domain
format to new domains in the new domain format.

The main difference between the formats is that the old domain format
has a description file while the new format has not.

The HELP command accepts as topic a string of characters that
"matches" any of the topics. It uses '-', '*' and '+' in special ways:

   '-'  is used in the same way as ordinary SINTRAN matching.

   '+'  is used to match any single character.

   '*'  is used to match any string (0 or more) of characters.

If '*' is used at the beginning of a string, only those topics ENDING
with the string will be displayed. If you want to find all items
CONTAINING a string, you must therefore place an asterisk both
before and after the string, e.g., *xxx*.

Currently, the following topics are defined:

   COMMENT (topic)                        NEW-DOMAIN-FORMAT (topic)
   CONVERT-DOMAIN (command)               OLD-DOMAIN-FORMAT (topic)
   EXIT (command)                         SHELL (topic)
   HELP (command and default topic)       SIBAS (topic)
   LIMITATIONS (topic)

In addition, the following shell facilities are described:

   %        (Shell comment)  NB: Use COMMENT as topic.
   @        (Shell SINTRAN III command invocation)

You may also use the shell built-in help: See the topic "shell" or
just press SHIFT+HELP when prompted for a command.

OLD-DOMAIN-FORMAT
                        *****  Old Domain Format  *****

The old domain format has a description file that stores bookkeeping
information about each domain and each segment for the user who owns
the description file.

The contents  of each segment is stored in three files with file types
:PSEG, :DSEG and :LINK.

The old domain format will gradually be phased out so you may want
to convert many of your domains to the new format
by using this convert domain program or by relinking/loading
using ND's LINKER that is released together with this
program.

Some programs are started by RT programs that do not recognize new domains.
Such programs should not be converted to the new domain format.
Do not attempt to convert:

  Sibas, version F or older
  Notis-DS, version D or older
  Notis-ID, version B or older.

NEW-DOMAIN-FORMAT

                       *****  New Domain Format  *****

The new domain format has no description file, instead each domain is
stored on a separate file with file type :DOM and the bookkeeping
information about it is stored in a header at the beginning of the
:DOM file. Each segment that is private for this domain is called a
slave segment and is stored in the domain file.

Thus a simple domain may be copied by using @COPY-FILE only.

If a segment is shared with other domains or for any other reason
cannot be considered a slave segment, it is called a free segment.
These are stored in separate :SEG files and the bookkeeping
information about them is stored at the beginning of the file where it
is stored.

SHELL
                             *****  Shell  *****

The ND-SHELL is used as the command processor for Convert-Domain. This
gives you access to many features provided by the shell. You can, for
example, see a log of all your keystrokes while running the program.
Since the LINKER also uses the ND-SHELL, the interface to CONVERT-
DOMAIN is very similar and has many of the same facilities as the
LINKER.

If, however, you write parameters on the command line, the SHELL will
not be used. This means that if you write

   @ND CONV-DOM DEST-DOM SOURCE-DOM

the ND-SHELL will not be used.

Help in the shell:

   You may either use the HELP command - see the topic HELP - or
   use the shell built-in HELP.

   To display the shell help you use the HELP key.

   Pressing SHIFT+HELP gives a list of all commands matching what
   you have typed in so far.

   Pressing HELP gives a description of the topic matching what
   you have typed in so far if it is unambiguous.

Refer to available documentation for the LINKER and ND SHELL for
further details.

EXIT
                              *****  Exit  *****

This command leaves the CONVERT-DOMAIN command processor.

CONVERT-DOMAIN
                         *****  Convert-Domain  *****

The CONVERT-DOMAIN command has 5 parameters:

CONVERT-DOMAIN <Destination domain>
               <Source domain>
               <Include linked segments (Yes,No)>
               <Display progress information (Yes,No)>
               <Force free segment number(s)>...

The dots indicate that the last parameter is repeated until
you give no value to it.

<Destination> and <Source> are mandatory, while the other parameters
are optional. <Destination> will accept an empty string (i.e. just CR).

1  <Destination> is the name of the destination domain that is in
   the new domain format. If the :DOM file does not exist, it will be
   created. If you want to prevent an existing :DOM file from being
   overwritten, you should enclose the name in double quotes.
   If the file already exists, you will get an error message and no
   conversion will take place.

   Occurrences of '$' in the destination name will be substituted with
   the source domain name. An empty string is equivalent to one '$'.

2  <Source> is the name of the source domain. This domain must be in
   the old domain format. There is no default name.

3  <Include linked Segments> is a parameter that is answered by YES or
   NO (NO is default). The parameter is optional so you won't be asked
   for it.

   If you respond with YES, all :SEG files needed to run the
   destination domain will be copied to the same user as the
   destination domain, and the destination domain will be linked to
   these segments.

   This is useful if you want to copy the domain to a floppy diskette
   and want all the :SEG files to reside on the same floppy.

   If you respond with NO (default), the destination domain will get
   links to :SEG files that may reside in other user areas than the
   destination domain itself. If you, for example, have a SOURCE-DOM
   that is linked to (LIB)LIB-SEG:SEG and user LIB
   already has LIB-SEG:SEG, the segment will not be converted. Instead,
   DEST-DOM will only link to (LIB)LIB-SEG:SEG. IF (LIB) has no
   LIB-SEG:SEG it will attempt to create a file (LIB)LIB-SEG:SEG and,
   if successful, will convert the segment onto this file. If not
   successful, it will try to create LIB-SEG:SEG on the same user as
   the destination domain and, if that won't work either, it will
   create the file under the current user and convert LIB-SEG to LIB-
   SEG:SEG.

   NOTE: If you do want a free segment to be placed on a particular
   user, e.g., a run-time library under user 'libraries' you MUST
   ensure that it is being placed there. One way to do this is
   to convert the "domain" in user libraries and thus produce a
   :SEG file (you probably do not need the :DOM file so you can delete
   it) PRIOR to converting any applications that are linked to
   this run-time segment.

4  <Progress Info?> You use this parameter to decide whether or not
   you want progress information displayed on the screen during
   conversion:

   YES (default) means that it will give information about what it
   will do next. For example, immediately before copying the debug
   part of segment number 3, you will see the following message:

      >> Converting debug part for segment 3 <<

   NO means that this information will not be displayed.

5.... <Force free segment numbers?>

   Convert domain tries to do a 'clever' guess if the segment should
   be free or not. However, you may want to force a segment to be
   free even when Convert domain puts it on a :DOM file. A typical
   example is when you convert Notis-WP and you later want to link
   Dialogue applications to Notis. To force the program to convert
   Notis-WP to a :SEG file, you can do this:

   CONVERT-DOMAIN $ NOTIS-WP,,,0:31

   Each segment will be put on a :SEG file, and you will get a
   Notis-WP:DOM file, too. The domain file will only consist
   of pointers to :SEG files! Alternatively, you could have written
   0-31 or 0..31 (all of them work).


COMMENT

                          *****  Comments - %  *****

This is used to write comments in SHELL JOBs as in the following
example:

      % Convert from source to destination
      CONVERT-DOMAIN destination source
@
               *****  Sintran-III Command Execution - @  *****

This is used to give SINTRAN commands when inside the convert domain
program:

      % Convert from source to dest, delete dest first.
      @DELETE-FILE destination:DOM
      CONVERT-DOMAIN destination source

LIMITATIONS
Some programs are started by RT programs that do not recognize new domains.
Such programs should not be converted to the new domain format.
Do not attempt to convert:

  Sibas, version F or older
  Notis-DS, version D or older
  Notis-ID, version B or older.

Some programs assume that they are executing in the old domain format.
Be wary of programs that have commands with a "domain name" parameter,
because they may be reading the description file at run-time. There
may also be programs, typically compilers, that read their :LINK file
at run-time to determine the addresses of routines.
Do not attempt to convert:

  ND-500 Basic, version B or older

Two programs consist of only :PSEG and :DSEG files, and have no
description file. They cannot be converted:

  ND-500/5000 Swapper
  Symbolic Debugger

SIBAS
                    Do NOT convert Sibas, version F

The Sibas domains SIB2A, SERV2A, etc., in version F of Sibas are
started by RT programs. Those RT programs do NOT recognize the new
domain format. By carefully editing the file (SIB2-SYS-F)ND500-RTLOAD-
F04:MODE, one can load the RT programs with newer libraries that
recognize the new domain format. That is too complicated to explain
here, and we advise most customers to NOT attempt to convert Sibas,
version F, to the new domain format.

                 You may convert Sibas-R, version A

The important rule to remember is that all applications that are
linked to Sibas must be in the same domain format as Sibas if they use
Sibas Message communication. If your application leaves a message for
Sibas in the file (SIB-500)SIBAS-MESSAGE:DSEG, and Sibas reads from
(SIB-500)SIBAS-MESSAGE:SEG, Sibas will never answer!

You can mix domain formats if you use XMSG Communication, that is,
Sibas backend to your own local machine. Example: An application in
the old domain format can access process 1 in the new domain format by
specifying 201 instead of 1, if the local machine is defined in
XMSG as *SIB2. (The same rule applies if an application is linked to
one version of Sibas and it wants to access a Sibas process in a
different version of Sibas.)

If a Sibas domain exists in both the new and old domain format, SIBRC
will always start the new domain format domain! If you want to
convert one process at a time, you should store the new domain format
Sibas domains on a user other than SIB-500, so you have the
possibility of starting either version.

You may convert Sibas-R, version A to the new domain format,
according to this 7-step procedure. This procedure will also work
if your Sibas-R, version A is linked to version F of Sibas-Message.

       @cc 1) Give the user SIB-500 about 1000 pages
       @LIST-REENTRANT CONVERT
       @cc Log in as user SIB-500
       @ND CONVERT-DOMAIN $ SIB-LIB
       @cc This will convert the Sibas library, and both message
       @cc segments.

       @cc 2) Create a SINTRAN user DOM-SIB-500 and give it pages
       @CC Log in as SINTRAN user DOM-SIB-500
       @ND CONVERT $ (SIB-500)SIBRP
       @ND CONVERT $ (SIB-500)SIBRS
       @CC Since the Sibas message segments already exist on user
       @CC SIB-500, new copies will not be created.

       @CC 3) If you have applications linked to NOTIS-WP, NOTIS-WP
       @CC must be converted to a :SEG file.
       @CC Log in as SINTRAN user DOMAIN
       @ND CONVERT $ NOTIS-WP,,,0:31

       @CC 4) Log in as a user where a Sibas application exists.
       @ND CONVERT $ MY-APPLICATION

       @CC 5) Log in as SINTRAN user SYSTEM to see if domains are OK:
       @ND-500
       PLACE (DOM-SIB-500)SIBRP
       LIST-ACTIVE-SEGMENTS
       PLACE (DOM-SIB-500)SIBRS
       LIST-ACTIVE-SEGMENTS
       PLACE (SOME-USER)MY-APPLICATION
       LIST-ACTIVE-SEGMENTS

       CC See that the Sibas Message segments are the same in all
       CC 3 cases.
       EXIT

       @CC 6) Edit the file (ND-OPERATION)SW-CONFIG, so that you
       @CC have at least these 2 lines (assume proc. 1 is new form.)
       @CC     SYSTEM-NAME : SIBAS
       @CC        SIBAS-FILES=1-DOM-SIB-500

       @CC 7) Stop SIBAS, process 1. When you restart it, it will
       @CC execute in the new domain format.


     Converting Applications Linked to the "Wrong Version" of Sibas

If you have applications linked to version F of Sibas, and you are
updating to a newer version of Sibas in the new domain format, you
can nevertheless convert those applications if you have the mode
files that created them:

       @cc 1) Edit each Linkage-Loader mode file so that all
       @cc LINK-SEGMENT's and FORCE-SEGMENT-LINK's to Sibas are
       @cc removed. Run the mode file again.

       @CC 2) Convert the incomplete domains you have made:
       @CC Some incomplete domains may be on product diskettes:
       @ND CONVERT $ MY-APPLICATION
       @ND CONVERT $ (2:FLOPPY-USER)NOTIS-RG-D

       @CC 3) Link the incomplete domains to Sibas
       @ND LINKER
       SET-ADVANCED
       APPEND-DOMAIN MY-APPLICATION
       LINK (SIB-500)SIB-LIB
       % That will also automatically LINK to message segments
       LIST-ENT UNDEFINED
       CLOSE N N
       APPEND-DOMAIN NOTIS-RG-D
       LINK (SIB-500)SIB-LIB
       LIST-ENT UNDEFINED
       EXIT

Some products without mode files can also be converted in a similar
way. The product diskettes contain old domain format domains without
links to Sibas. They are linked to Sibas at installation time, but
by starting over again with the product diskettes, you can convert
them before they are linked, and then LINK them to Sibas in the new
domain format.
